  "query_preview": "..(Saturday through Thursday)\nIssued at 153 PM EDT THU OCT 22 2026\n\nA blocky pattern will still hold at the start of the long term \nperiod, with an expansive and amplified midlevel ridge over the \ncenter of the country. To the west of the ridge, troughing \nassociated with a closed low initially located over California \nwill have little ability to progress east through early next week,\nwhen this disturbance lifts north and finally begins to flatten \nthe ridge. With this pattern in place, local conditions will vary \nlittle from day to day through Tuesday as surface high pressure \nremains nearby.\n\nThrough Tuesday, weather should remain sunny as high pressure \ndominates. Each afternoon, temperatures will rise to near or \nslightly above average for this time of year, with highs in the \nupper 60s to low 70s. Sunday does look to be the warmest day of \nthe period, as the ridge expands east somewhat and 500 mb heights \npeak in roughly the 90th percentile compared to climatology. Even \nso, temperatures should remain mild, perhaps peaking in the mid \n70s at most. Troughing off the Mid Atlantic coast this weekend \nwill prevent any eastward progression of this ridge, keeping \neastern Kentucky firmly east of the ridge axis, and by extension, \nthe highest geopotential heights. By Monday the ridge will begin \nflattening, with high temperatures falling a degree or two. \n\nAccompanying these pleasant temperatures will be plenty of \nsunshine. Skies should be nearly clear through the weekend, with \njust a slight uptick in upper level moisture by early next week as\nflow aloft begins to shift more westerly. With such dry air just \nabove the surface, NBM dewpoints are almost certainly overdone, \nparticularly in the afternoon hours. Thus, dewpoints were nudged \ndown into the low and mid 40s, much more in line with ensemble \nguidance. Overnight, this dry air, in addition to clear skies and \na weak pressure gradient, should allow for efficient radiational \ncooling. This will set the stage for ridge valley splits as \nvalleys fall into the upper 30s and ridgetop lows remain in the \nlow 40s through early next week. \n\nBy Tuesday, flow aloft will become more southwesterly as the ridge\nfinally breaks down and troughing begins to dig into the central\nCONUS. Meanwhile, a surface low will progress across the Midwest,\nwith a weak and poorly defined cold front extending to the\nsouthwest of this system. This front will attempt to sag toward\nour area somewhat, but with limited upper level support, it should\nlose steam and become more diffuse with time, and is not expected\nto make it to the Commonwealth. Thus, the forecast still remains\ndry, with the main change being a slight increase in cloud cover\nand highs cooling by a degree or two.",
